/*  run_i6name_overloads — $i6Name on an OVERLOADED static must name the routine that is emitted.

    This is the case $i6Name exists for: a static method's I6 name cannot be derived from the
    Beguile name alone, because the mangling gains discriminators once the name is overloaded. The
    first implementation computed the name itself with staticRoutineName and produced

        _bgl_Money_scale_int_int          (what $i6Name emitted)
        _bgl_Money_scale_2_int_int        (what was actually emitted, and what calls reference)

    — a routine that does not exist. The mangler adds an ARITY component that only runs when the
    overload set is settled, so $i6Name has to settle it first, exactly as an ordinary call site
    does before reading i6name.

    It went unnoticed because the original coverage was a BASELINE test: baselines compare emitted
    text and never reach Inform, so a dangling routine name looks identical to a good one. This
    tier runs the program, which is the only way a wrong name shows up as a failure.  */
// EXPECT_OUTPUT: two=12 one=10 direct=12
#beguilerSettings { target = z5; informName = "none"; }

class Money : object {
    int cents;
    static int scale(int a, int b){ return a * b; }
    static int scale(int a){ return a * 2; }
}

emitter int viaTwo(int a, int b){ $i6Name(Money.scale(int, int))($a, $b) }
emitter int viaOne(int a)       { $i6Name(Money.scale(int))($a) }

void main(){
    print("two=");    print(viaTwo(3, 4));
    print(" one=");   print(viaOne(5));
    print(" direct="); print(Money.scale(3, 4));   // the ordinary call, for comparison
}
